theory.intro:
Reflecting in the line $y = x$ swaps the coordinates of every point: $(a, b)$ becomes $(b, a)$. A graph and its reflection are mirror images in that line.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-1.4,0) -- (5.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.4) -- (0,5.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (-1.2,-1.2) -- (5.2,5.2);
\node[left] at (-1.25,-1.25) {$y = x$};
\draw[thick, blue, <->, domain=-1.2:5.2, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.5*\x + 3});
\draw[thick, red, <->, domain=2.4:5.4, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {2*\x - 6});
\fill (1,3.5) circle (2pt);
\fill (3.5,1) circle (2pt);
\node[below right] at (1.05,3.35) {$P(a, b)$};
\node[below right] at (3.55,0.85) {$P'(b, a)$};
\node[blue] at (1.4,4.7) {$y = f(x)$};
\node[red, left] at (2.35,-1.15) {image};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
The curve $y = f(x)$ has a turning point at $(-1, 4)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-3.8,0) -- (4.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3) -- (0,4.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (-2.5,-2.5) -- (4.5,4.5) node[above right] {$y = x$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-3.5:1.5, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {4 - (\x+1)*(\x+1)}) node[pos=0.15, left] {$y = f(x)$};
\fill (-1,4) circle (2pt);
\node[above] at (-1,4.1) {$(-1, 4)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
What are the coordinates of the corresponding turning point on the reflected graph?

development.question_text:
Show that the rectangular hyperbola $y = \dfrac{6}{x}$ is symmetric across the line $y = x$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-3.8,0) -- (3.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.8) -- (0,3.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (-3.4,-3.4) -- (3.4,3.4) node[above right] {$y = x$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=1.75:3.4286, samples=60, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {6/\x});
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-3.4286:-1.75, samples=60, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {6/\x});
\node[above left] at (1.85,3.5) {$y = \dfrac{6}{x}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
